{
  "id": "Compound::DB01586 - Disease::DOID:12236",
  "verbalization": "Ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA) is implicated in the pathogenesis of primary biliary cirrhosis (PBC) through a mechanism involving bile acid metabolism. The evidence suggests that UDCA may act as a modulator of bile acid synthesis and excretion, potentially influencing the balance of bile acids in the liver. This is supported by the observed relationship between UDCA and Chenodeoxycholic Acid (CDCA), which is known to play a role in bile acid homeostasis. CDCA is associated with the treatment of PBC, indicating a possible therapeutic role for UDCA in this condition. The shared pathway involving bile acids and the digestive system further strengthens the hypothesis that UDCA may exert its effects through a mechanism that involves bile acid modulation.\n\nThe evidence also highlights a potential link between UDCA and the treatment of PBC through the interaction with Chenodeoxycholic Acid, which is a key bile acid in the pathophysiology of the disease. The presence of a common ancestor between UDCA and CDCA, along with their shared role in bile acid metabolism, supports the idea that UDCA may influence bile acid homeostasis in the liver. This mechanism is consistent with the known role of bile acids in PBC, where dysregulation of bile acid metabolism is a key factor. The integration of these findings suggests that UDCA may have a therapeutic potential in PBC by modulating bile acid levels, although further experimental validation is needed to confirm this mechanism.",
